Abstract

Contact intelligence—the capacity of a robot to purposefully exploit physical interactions with its environment—demands a unified treatment of geometry, uncertainty, information, safety, and computation. This thesis develops the Future Contact Value (FCV) framework, a decision‑theoretic foundation that quantifies the long‑term value of a contact primitive as a single advantage function. We model the robot as a hybrid stochastic system evolving on a compact admissible configuration manifold; body velocities are decomposed via a rigorous Hodge–Helmholtz splitting that separates locomotion from shape change. The belief‑space partially observable Markov decision process is endowed with a reward that trades off task progress against information gain, energy expenditure, sensor usage, and computational cost. We prove nine central theorems: a geometric lower bound showing that contacts with large locomotion components are intrinsically valuable; a Wasserstein‑robustness bound guaranteeing performance under distributional ambiguity; an information‑theoretic decomposition that linearly lower‑bounds the value of exteroceptive and proprioceptive observations; submodularity of sensor selection, justifying a greedy sensor activation policy; a safety‑conditioned FCV obtained via hierarchical control barrier functions with an exact penalty; an integrated suboptimality bound that nests horizon truncation, value‑function approximation, and solver gaps; a macro‑action consistency theorem that justifies hierarchical planning; a two‑timescale offline‑online gap bound for complementarity‑free dynamics; and a main synthesis theorem assembling all error sources into a single performance guarantee. The framework is instantiated in a three‑layer computational architecture—offline IMPACT macro‑action generation, online reduced‑order complementarity‑free model predictive control, and a reactive CBF‑CLF quadratic program—whose per‑decision complexity is explicitly bounded. Together, the results establish a normative, resource‑aware calculus for contact intelligence, providing rigorous guarantees that are independent of any particular learning implementation and setting a mathematical foundation for physically interactive humanoid robots.

