Introduction

Contact is the defining characteristic of physical intelligence. Whether a humanoid climbs a ladder by grasping a rung, a quadruped wedges a foot into a crevice, or a manipulator slides a peg into a hole, the ability to deliberately make and break contacts is what distinguishes embodied agents from purely kinematic motion planners. Yet contact also introduces the most challenging aspects of robotics: hybrid dynamics with discontinuous jumps, non‑Gaussian and multiplicative uncertainty, abrupt changes in the geometry of feasible motion, and the ever‑present danger of catastrophic failure. For a robot operating in the real world, every contact decision must simultaneously answer four questions: Can I move in the direction I need? (geometry), What might go wrong if my model is off? (robustness), How much will I learn about the world? (information), and Is this safe and affordable? (resources). The robotics literature has treated these questions largely in isolation: geometric mechanics studies reachable body velocities, stochastic control provides robustness certificates, information theory quantifies sensing value, and control barrier functions enforce safety. A robot in the field, however, must fuse all of these considerations into a single, instantaneous choice of where to put its hands or feet. The absence of a principled, unified decision theory for contact has led to brittle, hand‑crafted contact schedules or, at the other extreme, purely data‑driven policies that lack guarantees.

This thesis introduces the Future Contact Value (FCV) framework, a decision‑centric theory that synthesises the four questions above into a scalar advantage function—the amount by which a candidate contact improves (or worsens) the robot’s long‑term prospects, net of all costs. The central object is a partially observable Markov decision process (POMDP) defined over a hybrid belief state; the reward function explicitly encodes task progress, an information bonus weighted by belief entropy, and penalties for energy, sensing, and computation. The optimal value function V^* satisfies the Bellman equation, and the Future Contact Value of a contact primitive c at belief b is defined as

\operatorname{FCV}(b,c) = \max_{a\in\mathcal{A}_c(b)} Q^*(b,c,a) - V^*(b),

where Q^* is the state‑action value function. By construction, \max_c \operatorname{FCV}(b,c)=0 and the optimal contact attains zero; FCV thus measures how much a contact deviates from optimality. Maximising FCV greedily yields a principled contact selection policy, and the bulk of the thesis is devoted to proving that this greedy policy is near‑optimal, with every source of suboptimality explicitly bounded.

The mathematical backbone rests on seven pillars—differential geometry, stochastic processes, information theory, variational optimization, stability theory, optimal control, and trajectory optimization—integrated hierarchically. After establishing rigorous definitions for the hybrid state, the contact manifold, and the belief dynamics (formulated as a switching diffusion to correctly handle mode jumps), the thesis proceeds through a sequence of theorems that each isolate a fundamental component of FCV.

Geometry. On the admissible configuration manifold, every contact c defines a linear contact map \mathbf{A}_c from joint velocities to body velocities. The image of \mathbf{A}_c is the subspace of instantaneously achievable motions. We employ a Hodge–Helmholtz decomposition that splits any body velocity into a locomotion component (the gradient of a potential on shape space) and a divergence‑free shape‑change component. Theorem 1 proves that the task reward achievable under contact c is lower‑bounded by the maximal locomotion projection along the task direction; thus contacts that span large locomotion components are geometrically valuable, independently of any value‑function approximation.

Robustness. Real contact interactions generate non‑Gaussian, multiplicative uncertainty. We capture this by a Wasserstein ambiguity set around the nominal belief transition. Theorem 2 shows that the robust FCV, which takes the worst‑case expected future value over this set, is at most \gamma L\epsilon worse than the nominal FCV, where L is the Lipschitz constant of the value function and \epsilon the ambiguity radius. This penalises contacts whose outcomes are highly sensitive to model mismatch.

Information. Observations made during a contact reduce both environmental uncertainty (exteroceptive) and self‑state uncertainty (proprioceptive). Theorem 3 decomposes the expected value gain linearly into terms proportional to mutual information, with constants \kappa_{\text{ext}}, \kappa_{\text{prop}} defined via infima of the value‑of‑information ratio. The mutual information itself is submodular in the set of activated sensors (Theorem 4), directly justifying a greedy sensor activation policy that implements the Belief‑Conditioned One‑step Diffusion (B‑COD) principle: activate only the most informative sensors until uncertainty drops below a safety threshold.

Safety. A three‑layer safety filtration—physical limits, force constraints, and belief covariance bounds—is enforced via control barrier functions (CBFs). Rather than assigning infinite penalty to unsafe contacts, which causes numerical problems, we use an exact penalty formulation (Theorem 5): \operatorname{FCV}(b,c) = \operatorname{FCV}_{\text{safe}}(b,c) - M\cdot\mathbb{I}[\text{CBF infeasible}], with a finite M that recovers the hard‑constrained optimum.

Suboptimality bounds. In practice, FCV is approximated with a learned value network, a complementarity‑free contact solver, and a finite lookahead. Theorem 6 provides a nested decomposition of the resulting performance loss, separating horizon truncation, value‑function error, and solver gap, with the advantage approximation error compounded as (1-\gamma)^{-2} following approximate policy iteration theory. The macro‑action framework is shown to be consistent with the primitive POMDP (Theorem 7), and the gap between offline exact‑dynamics planning and online complementarity‑free execution is bounded explicitly (Theorem 8).

Synthesis. The main result, Theorem 9, assembles all error sources into a single performance guarantee: the value of the greedy FCV policy is within an additive gap \Delta_{\text{horizon}}+\Delta_{\text{approx}}+\Delta_{\text{solver}}+\Delta_{\text{robust}}+\Delta_{\text{info}} of the optimal value, with every term given in closed form.

The final chapter bridges theory to implementation: we describe a three‑timescale architecture in which macro‑actions are pre‑computed offline using the IMPACT solver, reduced‑order complementarity‑free model predictive control runs online at 10–100 Hz, and a reactive CBF‑CLF quadratic program enforces safety at 1 kHz. The per‑decision computational complexity is shown to be O(|\mathcal{C}_{\text{safe}}|\cdot H \cdot T_{\text{MPC}}), where |\mathcal{C}_{\text{safe}}| is the number of safe contacts, H the lookahead, and T_{\text{MPC}} the solve time of the online optimizer—placing the method within the reach of current humanoid hardware.

The core contribution is a normative standard for contact intelligence: FCV is not simply a hand‑crafted heuristic or a black‑box learned policy, but an advantage function derived from first principles of physics, information, and computation, accompanied by rigorous bounds that hold irrespective of the particular approximation method. This provides both a practical algorithm for contact selection and a theoretical benchmark against which any contact decision‑maker can be evaluated. The thesis positions FCV as a unifying bridge between the analytic guarantees of model‑based robotics and the scalability of learning‑based approaches, laying the mathematical foundation for the next generation of physically interactive machines.


1. Axiomatic Foundations and Scope

Contact intelligence, the capacity of a robot to purposefully use physical interaction with its environment, is governed by five irreducible constraints:

(A1) Physical geometry. Every contact defines a kinematic map from joint velocities to body velocities; only a subspace of SE(3) motions is instantaneously accessible.
(A2) Resource bounds. Energy, sensing, and computation are finite; every contact imposes a cost in these three currencies.
(A3) Epistemic uncertainty. The robot must maintain a belief over hybrid (continuous‑discrete) states and environmental parameters.
(A4) Safety. Joint limits, friction cones, and estimation accuracy must be respected at all times.
(A5) Multiscale dynamics. Contact phenomena range from continuous force‑motion laws to discrete impact events, demanding a hybrid system description.

The FCV framework translates these axioms into a single scalar function—the advantage of choosing a contact primitive—and provides normative guarantees on the performance of a greedy policy that maximizes FCV.

