2. Hybrid State, Contact Manifold, and Belief Dynamics

The robot’s physical state is

x = (q,\dot q, m) \in \mathcal{X} = \mathcal{Q} \times T\mathcal{Q} \times \mathcal{M},

where \mathcal{Q} is the configuration manifold, T\mathcal{Q} its tangent bundle, and \mathcal{M} a finite set of contact modes. The admissible configuration set \mathcal{Q}_{\text{adm}}\subset\mathcal{Q} is compact, bounded by torque and joint‑limit constraints. A contact primitive c\in\mathcal{C} is a parameterized affordance (e.g., “grasp rail at height h”). We assume \mathcal{C} is a finite set, or, if \mathcal{C} is continuous, that \operatorname{FCV}(b,c)\to -\infty as c leaves every compact subset; this guarantees the maxima in what follows are attained.

The dynamics follow a switching diffusion (a Piecewise‑Deterministic Markov Process) that cleanly separates continuous motion and discrete jumps:

dx = F_{m(t)}(x,a)\,dt + G_{m(t)}(x)\,dw + \sum_{k} \Delta x_k \, dN_k(t),

with continuous action a\in\mathcal{A}_c(x) (joint torques admissible under contact c), a Wiener process w, and Poisson counting processes N_k(t) for contact make/break events. Jump amplitudes \Delta x_k capture the instantaneous velocity resets imposed by impact laws. This formulation, standard in hybrid control theory (Bensoussan & Menaldi, 1997; Yuan & Mao, 2003), makes the subsequent Bayesian filtering well‑posed.

The robot receives observations z = (z_{\text{ext}},z_{\text{prop}}) from exteroceptive and proprioceptive sensors. The belief state b\in\mathcal{B} is a probability density over \mathcal{X}, updated recursively by the Bayesian filter

b' = \tau(b,c,a,z).

In continuous time, the belief evolves according to the stochastic differential equation

db = \mathcal{L}_F b\,dt + \mathcal{L}_G b\,dw,

augmented by jump corrections, as employed in the continuous‑time Gaussian belief trees of Mazouz et al. (2026); their belief‑barrier safety checker is adapted in §6.

Contact space geometry. Each contact c induces a linear map \mathbf{A}_c : T_q\mathcal{Q} \to \mathfrak{se}(3) from shape velocities to body velocities; its image V_c = \operatorname{Im}(\mathbf{A}_c) is the subspace of instantaneously achievable body velocities. Because \mathcal{Q}_{\text{adm}} is compact, we decompose body velocities using the Hodge–Helmholtz splitting on the admissible manifold: on the translational part \mathbb{R}^3 we apply the classical Helmholtz decomposition, and on the rotational part \mathfrak{so}(3)\cong\mathbb{R}^3—the Lie algebra of the compact group SO(3)—we apply the Hodge decomposition for 1‑forms. This yields a unique orthogonal splitting

\mathbf{v} = \mathbf{v}_{\text{loc}} + \mathbf{v}_{\text{curl}},

where \mathbf{v}_{\text{loc}} is the locomotion component (the horizontal lift of the base curve in the principal bundle \mathcal{Q} \to SE(3), i.e., the gradient of a potential on shape space) and \mathbf{v}_{\text{curl}} is the shape‑change component (divergence‑free). The decomposition is grounded in geometric mechanics (Kelly & Murray, 1995; Chong et al., 2023/2024).

