3. The Future Contact Value as an Advantage Function

The planning problem is a constrained partially observable Markov decision process (POMDP). Over beliefs it reduces to a fully observable MDP with composite actions u=(c,a). The instantaneous reward includes task reward, an information bonus, and resource penalties:

\begin{aligned}
r(b,c,a) = &\; R_{\text{task}}(b,a) + \beta(b)\, I(X'; Z \mid b,c) \\
& - \lambda_E \Delta E(c) - \lambda_S \Delta\mathcal{S}(c) - \lambda_{\mathcal{K}} \Delta\mathcal{K}(c),
\end{aligned}

where \beta(b) increases with belief entropy, encouraging exploration when uncertainty is high. The optimal value function V^* satisfies the Bellman equation

V^*(b) = \max_{c\in\mathcal{C}}\max_{a\in\mathcal{A}_c(b)} \big\{ r(b,c,a) + \gamma\,\mathbb{E}_{z}[ V^*(\tau(b,c,a,z)) ] \big\}. \tag{1}

Definition (Future Contact Value). The FCV of a primitive c is the advantage function

\boxed{\operatorname{FCV}(b, c) = \max_{a \in \mathcal{A}_c(b)} Q^*(b, c, a) \;-\; V^*(b)}, \tag{2}

where Q^*(b,c,a) = r(b,c,a) + \gamma\,\mathbb{E}_z[ V^*(\tau(b,c,a,z)) ]. By construction, \max_c \operatorname{FCV}(b,c) = 0 and the optimal contact c^*(b) attains zero. FCV quantifies how much better (or worse) than average a contact is, net of all costs, exactly in the units of the value function.

