4. Geometric Reachability Bound

Assume the task reward is strictly increasing in the projection of the expected body velocity onto a fixed direction \mathbf{d}\in\mathfrak{se}(3).

Theorem 1 (Geometric Lower Bound).
Let \mathcal{V}_{\text{adm}} be the set of body velocities admissible under torque and stability limits. Then

\max_{a\in\mathcal{A}_c(b)} R_{\text{task}}(b,a) \;\ge\; R_{\text{task,max}}\!\left( \max_{\mathbf{v}\in V_c \cap \mathcal{V}_{\text{adm}}} \langle \mathbf{v}, \mathbf{d}\rangle \right). \tag{3}

Moreover,

\max_{\mathbf{v}\in V_c \cap \mathcal{V}_{\text{adm}}} \langle \mathbf{v}, \mathbf{d}\rangle \;\ge\; \max_{\mathbf{v}\in V_c \cap \mathcal{V}_{\text{adm}}} \langle \mathbf{v}_{\text{loc}}, \mathbf{d}\rangle.

Proof. The map from admissible joint torques to joint velocities has dense image in \mathcal{V}_{\text{adm}} under generic full‑rank conditions; hence any \mathbf{v} in the feasible set can be approximated arbitrarily well by a suitable action. Monotonicity and continuity of the reward give the first inequality. The Hodge–Helmholtz decomposition is orthogonal with respect to the kinetic energy metric, so \langle \mathbf{v}, \mathbf{d}\rangle = \langle \mathbf{v}_{\text{loc}}, \mathbf{d}\rangle + \langle \mathbf{v}_{\text{curl}}, \mathbf{d}\rangle. Restricting the maximisation to the subspace \mathbf{v}_{\text{curl}}=0 yields the second bound. ∎

This theorem provides a purely geometric criterion: contacts whose contact maps span large locomotion components in task‑relevant directions are inherently valuable, independent of any value function approximation.

