5. Robust FCV Under Distributional Uncertainty

Real contact interactions introduce non‑Gaussian, multiplicative uncertainty. We capture this with a Wasserstein ambiguity set.

Definition. For a nominal belief transition \hat p(\cdot\mid b,c,a), the robust transition set is

\mathfrak{P}(b,c,a) = \{\, p \in \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{B}) \mid W_\rho(p, \hat p) \le \epsilon \,\},

with radius \epsilon>0. The robust FCV is

\operatorname{FCV}_{\text{rob}}(b,c) = \max_{a} \inf_{p\in\mathfrak{P}(b,c,a)} \big( r(b,c,a) + \gamma\, \mathbb{E}_{b'\sim p}[V^*(b')] \big) - V^*(b). \tag{4}

Theorem 2 (Robust Lower Bound).
Assume V^* is L-Lipschitz continuous with respect to the Wasserstein‑1 distance (hence any W_\rho for \rho\ge1). Then

\operatorname{FCV}_{\text{rob}}(b,c) \;\ge\; \operatorname{FCV}_{\text{nom}}(b,c) \;-\; \gamma L \epsilon,

where \operatorname{FCV}_{\text{nom}} is computed with the nominal transition \hat p.

Proof. Kantorovich–Rubinstein duality gives \inf_{p: W_1\le\epsilon} \mathbb{E}_p[V^*] \ge \mathbb{E}_{\hat p}[V^*] - L\epsilon. Substituting into the definition of FCV directly yields the inequality. ∎

Thus, even under severe model misspecification, the conservative FCV penalizes each contact by an amount proportional to the ambiguity radius and the Lipschitz constant of the value function.

