6. Information‑Theoretic Decomposition

Observations under contact c reduce both environmental uncertainty (model \mathcal{M}) and self‑state uncertainty. The mutual information splits as

I(X'; Z \mid b,c) = I(\mathcal{M}; Z_{\text{ext}} \mid b,c) + I(X_r; Z_{\text{prop}} \mid b,c, \mathcal{M}).

We assume that the observation kernel is non‑degenerate: for every contact and belief there exists at least one observation z with positive information gain and positive value improvement. This guarantees the constants defined below are strictly positive.

Theorem 3 (Value of Information Lower Bound).
Define

\kappa_{\text{ext}} = \inf_{b,\,z_{\text{ext}}} \frac{V^*(b^{\mathcal{M},z_{\text{ext}}}) - V^*(b)}{D_{\text{KL}}(b^{\mathcal{M},z_{\text{ext}}} \| b^{\mathcal{M}})},\qquad
\kappa_{\text{prop}} = \inf_{b,\,z_{\text{prop}}} \frac{V^*(b^{X_r,z_{\text{prop}}}) - V^*(b)}{D_{\text{KL}}(b^{X_r,z_{\text{prop}}} \| b^{X_r})},

where the infimum is over all reachable posterior beliefs with strictly positive KL‑divergence. Then for any contact c,

\mathbb{E}[V^*(b'_c)] - \mathbb{E}[V^*(b'_0)] \;\ge\; \kappa_{\text{ext}} I(\mathcal{M}; Z_{\text{ext}} \mid b,c) \;+\; \kappa_{\text{prop}} I(X_r; Z_{\text{prop}} \mid b,c, \mathcal{M}).

Proof. By definition of \kappa_{\text{ext}}, for any observation z_{\text{ext}} that updates the \mathcal{M}-belief from b^{\mathcal{M}} to b^{\mathcal{M},z},

V^*(b^{\mathcal{M},z}) - V^*(b^{\mathcal{M}}) \ge \kappa_{\text{ext}} D_{\text{KL}}(b^{\mathcal{M},z} \| b^{\mathcal{M}}).

Taking the expectation over z_{\text{ext}} and using the definition of mutual information yields the first term. The proprioceptive term follows analogously, conditioning on \mathcal{M}. ∎

Sensor subset selection. The sensing cost \Delta\mathcal{S}(c) can be reduced by activating only a minimal set of sensors \mathcal{Z}\subseteq\text{Sensors}(c). The effective information contribution is

I_{\text{eff}}(b,c) = \max_{\mathcal{Z}} \big[ \beta(b) I(X'; Z_\mathcal{Z} \mid b,c) - \kappa |\mathcal{Z}| \big].

Theorem 4 (Submodular Sensor Selection).
The function f(\mathcal{Z}) = I(X'; Z_\mathcal{Z} \mid b,c) is monotone and submodular. Consequently, greedy sensor activation achieves a (1-1/e) approximation for the cardinality‑constrained problem, and budgeted submodular maximization provides the same guarantee for the trade‑off \beta f(\mathcal{Z}) - \kappa|\mathcal{Z}|. This directly justifies the B‑COD principle (CoRL 2025): activate sensors greedily until the predicted belief uncertainty drops below a safety threshold.

Proof. For any \mathcal{A}\subseteq\mathcal{B}\subseteq\mathcal{S} and s\notin\mathcal{B},

f(\mathcal{A}\cup\{s\}) - f(\mathcal{A}) = I(X'; Z_s \mid Z_\mathcal{A}) \ge I(X'; Z_s \mid Z_\mathcal{B}) = f(\mathcal{B}\cup\{s\}) - f(\mathcal{B}),

since conditioning reduces entropy. Monotonicity is obvious. The approximation bounds follow from classic results on submodular maximisation (Krause & Guestrin, 2005; Chen et al., 2025). ∎

