7. Safety‑Guaranteed FCV via Hierarchical Barrier Functions

Safety is enforced through three continuously differentiable control barrier functions (CBFs):

· Physical: h_{\text{phys}}(x)\ge 0 (joint limits, self‑collision, centre of mass within support polygon).
· Force: h_{\text{force}}(x,c)\ge 0 (friction cone, contact force limits; may require high‑order CBFs, as in Stölzle, 2025).
· Belief: h_{\text{belief}}(b)\ge 0 (trace of the estimation error covariance below a maximum \sigma_{\max}^2; forward invariance is checked using the continuous‑time belief dynamics from Mazouz et al., 2026).

A contact c is safe from belief b if the combined CBF‑CLF quadratic program is feasible. To avoid mathematically problematic infinite values, we impose a large but finite penalty:

\operatorname{FCV}(b,c) = \operatorname{FCV}_{\text{safe}}(b,c) \;-\; M \cdot \mathbb{I}[\text{CBF infeasible at } b],

with M>0. Exact penalty theory (Bertsekas, 2014) guarantees that a finite M^* exists such that for all M\ge M^*, the optimal policy of the penalised problem coincides with the hard‑constrained optimal policy.

Theorem 5 (Safety‑Conditioned FCV).
For safe contacts (CBF‑QP feasible), \operatorname{FCV}_{\text{safe}}(b,c) equals the optimal value of the constrained POMDP where actions at each step are restricted to those satisfying the CBF constraints. For unsafe contacts, the penalized FCV discourages their selection without introducing infinite values.

Proof. The constrained MDP can be exactly reformulated as an unconstrained one with penalty M \cdot \mathbb{I}[\text{infeasible}]; optimality of the penalised formulation for large M follows from exact penalty theory for constrained MDPs. ∎

