8. Integrated Suboptimality Under Multiple Approximations

In practice, FCV is computed with (i) an approximate value network \widetilde V yielding \widetilde{\operatorname{FCV}} with error \varepsilon_{\text{FCV}}, (ii) a complementarity‑free contact solver (Jin, 2025) introducing a solver optimality gap \varepsilon_{\text{solver}}, and (iii) planning limited to H macro‑steps of maximum primitive length L_{\max}.

Theorem 6 (Nested Suboptimality Bound).
The greedy policy \tilde\pi with respect to \widetilde{\operatorname{FCV}} satisfies

V^{\tilde\pi}(b) \;\ge\; V^*(b) \;-\; \frac{2\gamma^{H L_{\max}} R_{\max}}{(1-\gamma)^2} \;-\; \frac{2\varepsilon_{\text{FCV}}}{(1-\gamma)^2} \;-\; \frac{\varepsilon_{\text{solver}}}{1-\gamma},

where R_{\max} is the maximum absolute instantaneous reward.

Proof. We decompose the error into three independent sources.

1. Horizon truncation. The receding‑horizon policy with lookahead H (using true values) loses at most \frac{2\gamma^{H L_{\max}} R_{\max}}{(1-\gamma)^2} relative to the infinite‑horizon optimum (standard approximate dynamic programming bound).
2. Advantage approximation. Within the H-step lookahead, the greedy policy selects macro‑actions based on \widetilde{\operatorname{FCV}} instead of the true advantage. The approximate advantage is used both for evaluating the lookahead value and for choosing the immediate contact; this mirrors approximate policy iteration, where approximation errors compound through evaluation and improvement, leading to a performance loss bounded by \frac{2\varepsilon_{\text{FCV}}}{(1-\gamma)^2} (Munos, 2003).
3. Solver suboptimality. If the trajectory optimiser returns an action \tilde a with Q^*(b,c,\tilde a) \ge Q^*(b,c,a^*) - \varepsilon_{\text{solver}}, the one‑step value gap is at most \varepsilon_{\text{solver}}. Over the infinite horizon this accumulates to at most \frac{\varepsilon_{\text{solver}}}{1-\gamma}.

The three errors act on disjoint aspects of the decision process; summing them gives the claimed bound. ∎

