10. Main Synthesis Theorem

Assembling all error sources, we obtain a single performance guarantee for the FCV‑based greedy policy.

Theorem 9 (Synthesis: FCV Performance Guarantee).
Let \tilde\pi be the greedy policy using online FCV with horizon H, solver gap \varepsilon_{\text{solver}}, FCV approximation error \varepsilon_{\text{FCV}}, Wasserstein ambiguity \epsilon, and sensor subset chosen greedily. Then

V^{\tilde\pi}(b) \;\ge\; V^*(b) \;-\; \Delta_{\text{horizon}} \;-\; \Delta_{\text{approx}} \;-\; \Delta_{\text{solver}} \;-\; \Delta_{\text{robust}} \;-\; \Delta_{\text{info}},

where

\begin{aligned}
\Delta_{\text{horizon}} &= \frac{2\gamma^{H L_{\max}} R_{\max}}{(1-\gamma)^2}, \\[2mm]
\Delta_{\text{approx}} &= \frac{2\varepsilon_{\text{FCV}}}{(1-\gamma)^2}, \\[2mm]
\Delta_{\text{solver}} &= \frac{\varepsilon_{\text{solver}}}{1-\gamma}, \\[2mm]
\Delta_{\text{robust}} &= \frac{\gamma L \epsilon}{1-\gamma}, \\[2mm]
\Delta_{\text{info}} &\le \kappa_{\text{ext}}\bigl( I_{\mathcal{M}}^{\max} - I_{\mathcal{M}}^{\text{greedy}} \bigr) + \kappa_{\text{prop}}\bigl( I_{X_r}^{\max} - I_{X_r}^{\text{greedy}} \bigr).
\end{aligned}

Proof. Theorems 1–8 bound each component individually; because the suboptimalities arise from independent approximations (planning horizon, value function quality, solver accuracy, model robustness, and sensing sufficiency), the total loss is bounded by the sum of the individual losses. ∎

