11. Computational Complexity and Resource Model

Computing \operatorname{FCV}(b,c) exactly is a POMDP problem, which is PSPACE‑complete in general. The hierarchical FCV architecture reduces the per‑decision complexity to a tractable level:

· Offline: building the macro‑action library costs O(|\mathcal{M}| \cdot T_{\text{IMPACT}}), where T_{\text{IMPACT}} is the solve time for one macro‑action under exact LCP dynamics; this is a one‑time, amortised cost.
· Online MPC: for a given belief, the cost is O(|\mathcal{C}_{\text{safe}}| \cdot H \cdot T_{\text{MPC}}), where |\mathcal{C}_{\text{safe}}| is the number of contacts surviving the safety filter, H the lookahead length, and T_{\text{MPC}} the time for one complementarity‑free CI‑MPC solve (a few milliseconds).
· Reactive layer: the CBF‑CLF‑QP is solved at 1 kHz, with cost independent of the size of the contact library.

Sensor selection adds O(|\text{Sensors}| \log |\text{Sensors}|) using greedy maximisation. The overall architecture thus respects the bounded‑computation axiom.

