Appendix A. Proof of Theorem 1 (Geometric Reachability Bound)

Theorem 1. Let V_c = \operatorname{Im}(\mathbf{A}_c) and \mathcal{V}_{\text{adm}} the set of body velocities admissible under torque and stability constraints on the compact admissible configuration manifold \mathcal{Q}_{\text{adm}}. Assume R_{\text{task}}(b,a) is strictly increasing in the projection \langle \mathbf{v}, \mathbf{d}\rangle of the expected body velocity onto a fixed direction \mathbf{d}\in\mathfrak{se}(3). Then

\max_{a\in\mathcal{A}_c(b)} R_{\text{task}}(b,a) \ge R_{\text{task,max}}\!\left( \max_{\mathbf{v}\in V_c \cap \mathcal{V}_{\text{adm}}} \langle \mathbf{v}, \mathbf{d}\rangle \right).

Moreover, decomposing \mathbf{v} = \mathbf{v}_{\text{loc}} + \mathbf{v}_{\text{curl}} via the Hodge–Helmholtz splitting on \mathcal{Q}_{\text{adm}} gives

\max_{\mathbf{v}\in V_c \cap \mathcal{V}_{\text{adm}}} \langle \mathbf{v}, \mathbf{d}\rangle \ge \max_{\mathbf{v}\in V_c \cap \mathcal{V}_{\text{adm}}} \langle \mathbf{v}_{\text{loc}}, \mathbf{d}\rangle .

Proof. The mapping from admissible joint torques to joint velocities is the inverse of the inertia matrix plus Coriolis terms, which, under the full‑rank assumption on \mathcal{Q}_{\text{adm}}, is a linear isomorphism between the torque space and the tangent space of the submanifold of reachable configurations. Hence for any \mathbf{v} \in V_c \cap \mathcal{V}_{\text{adm}} there exists a sequence of torques, and thus actions, that produces velocities arbitrarily close to \mathbf{v}. Continuity and monotonicity of R_{\text{task}} then yield the first inequality.

For the decomposition, we restrict attention to the compact submanifold \mathcal{Q}_{\text{adm}}, on which the contact map defines a smooth vector bundle. On the translational part \mathbb{R}^3 we apply the classical Helmholtz theorem: any smooth vector field can be uniquely split as \mathbf{v}_{\text{trans}} = \nabla \phi_{\text{trans}} + \nabla \times \boldsymbol{\psi}_{\text{trans}}. On the rotational part \mathfrak{so}(3), identified with \mathbb{R}^3 but equipped with the bi‑invariant metric of SO(3), the Hodge decomposition applies because SO(3) is a compact Riemannian manifold without boundary. Thus the rotational velocity 1‑form decomposes as \mathbf{v}_{\text{rot}} = d f_{\text{rot}} + \delta \beta with f_{\text{rot}} a function and \beta a 2‑form. The combined decomposition is orthogonal with respect to the kinetic energy metric, and we denote \mathbf{v}_{\text{loc}} the component lying in the exact (gradient) subspace and \mathbf{v}_{\text{curl}} the complementary (co‑exact/divergence‑free) part. Orthogonality implies \langle \mathbf{v}, \mathbf{d}\rangle = \langle \mathbf{v}_{\text{loc}}, \mathbf{d}\rangle + \langle \mathbf{v}_{\text{curl}}, \mathbf{d}\rangle. Restricting the maximisation to the subspace \mathbf{v}_{\text{curl}} = 0 yields a lower bound. \square

