Appendix G. Proof of Theorem 7 (Macro‑Action Consistency)

Theorem 7. If the macro‑action library \mathcal{M} contains all primitive sequences of length up to L_{\max}, then V_{\text{macro}}^* = V^* and the greedy macro‑action policy achieves V^*.

Proof. The primitive Bellman operator \mathcal{T} is

(\mathcal{T}V)(b) = \max_{c,a} \big[ r(b,c,a) + \gamma \mathbb{E}[V(b')] \big].

Unrolling L steps yields

(\mathcal{T}^L V)(b) = \max_{c_{0:L-1}, a_{0:L-1}} \mathbb{E}\Big[ \sum_{t=0}^{L-1} \gamma^t r_t + \gamma^L V(b_L) \Big].

By definition, a macro‑action of length L is an open‑loop policy of L steps, so the macro‑action Bellman operator \mathcal{T}_{\text{macro}} coincides with \mathcal{T}^{L_{\max}} when the library contains all sequences of length \le L_{\max}. The primitive value function V^* is the unique fixed point of \mathcal{T}, and therefore also of \mathcal{T}^L for any L because \mathcal{T}V^* = V^* implies \mathcal{T}^L V^* = V^*. Hence V^* satisfies the macro‑action Bellman equation and V_{\text{macro}}^* = V^*. The greedy macro‑action policy selects the macro‑action attaining the maximum in this equation; executing it gives a primitive policy that attains V^*. \square

