Appendix H. Proof of Theorem 8 (Two‑Timescale FCV Gap)

Theorem 8. Suppose the true transition kernel P^* (exact LCP) and the complementarity‑free approximation \widehat P satisfy \|P^*(\cdot\mid b,c,a) - \widehat P(\cdot\mid b,c,a)\|_{\text{TV}} \le \delta_{\text{cf}} for all (b,c,a). If V^* is L_V-Lipschitz with respect to the state distribution in total variation, then

|V^{\text{offline}} - V^{\text{online}}| \le \frac{\gamma L_V}{1-\gamma} \delta_{\text{cf}} .

Proof. The two value functions satisfy

V^{\text{offline}} = r + \gamma P^* V^{\text{offline}}, \qquad
V^{\text{online}} = r + \gamma \widehat P V^{\text{online}} .

Subtracting and taking supremum norms,

\begin{aligned}
\|V^{\text{offline}} - V^{\text{online}}\|_\infty
&\le \gamma \|P^* V^{\text{offline}} - \widehat P V^{\text{online}}\|_\infty \\
&\le \gamma \bigl( \|P^* V^{\text{offline}} - P^* V^{\text{online}}\|_\infty + \|P^* V^{\text{online}} - \widehat P V^{\text{online}}\|_\infty \bigr) \\
&\le \gamma \|V^{\text{offline}} - V^{\text{online}}\|_\infty + \gamma L_V \delta_{\text{cf}} .
\end{aligned}

The inequality \|P^* V^{\text{online}} - \widehat P V^{\text{online}}\|_\infty \le L_V \delta_{\text{cf}} holds because the value function is L_V-Lipschitz and the distributions differ by at most \delta_{\text{cf}} in total variation. Solving for the norm difference gives the stated bound. \square

---

Appendix I. Proof of Theorem 9 (Main Synthesis Theorem)

Theorem 9. Let \tilde\pi be the greedy policy with online FCV under all approximations. Then

V^{\tilde\pi}(b) \ge V^*(b) - \Delta_{\text{horizon}} - \Delta_{\text{approx}} - \Delta_{\text{solver}} - \Delta_{\text{robust}} - \Delta_{\text{info}},

with the explicit bounds stated in the theorem.

Proof. The total performance loss is the sum of losses from five independent sources:

1. Horizon truncation: \Delta_{\text{horizon}} from Theorem 6.
2. Value function approximation: \Delta_{\text{approx}} from Theorem 6.
3. Solver gap: \Delta_{\text{solver}} from Theorem 6.
4. Distributional robustness: Applying the robust FCV (Theorem 2) at every step adds a one‑step penalty \gamma L\epsilon. Over the infinite horizon the cumulative loss is \frac{\gamma L\epsilon}{1-\gamma}.
5. Information loss: The greedy sensor selection (Theorem 4) obtains mutual information I^{\text{greedy}} instead of the optimal I^{\max} achievable by full sensor activation. By Theorem 3, the value loss from missing information is bounded by \kappa_{\text{ext}}(I_{\mathcal{M}}^{\max} - I_{\mathcal{M}}^{\text{greedy}}) + \kappa_{\text{prop}}(I_{X_r}^{\max} - I_{X_r}^{\text{greedy}}).

Each contribution is derived from a distinct approximation and they combine additively because the errors affect non‑overlapping aspects of the decision process. \square

