Appendix J. Supporting Lemmas

J.1. Lipschitz continuity of V^*. Under bounded reward R_{\max}, discount factor \gamma<1, and transition kernel K-Lipschitz in belief with respect to Wasserstein‑1 distance, the optimal value function is L-Lipschitz with

L = \frac{R_{\max} K}{(1-\gamma)^2}.

Proof. The Bellman operator \mathcal{T} is a contraction in the Lipschitz norm; iterating from any initial Lipschitz function shows the fixed point inherits the smoothness. The (1-\gamma)^{-2} factor arises from the geometric series of error propagation through value iteration steps.

J.2. Hodge decomposition on SO(3). For a compact Lie group SO(3) with bi‑invariant metric, the Hodge theorem guarantees that any 1‑form \omega can be uniquely decomposed as \omega = d f + \delta \beta, where f is a function and \beta a 2‑form. This is the exact–co‑exact splitting used in the text.

J.3. Kantorovich–Rubinstein duality. For any Polish space and cost c(x,y) = \|x-y\|,

W_1(p,q) = \sup_{\phi: \text{Lip}(\phi)\le 1} \big( \mathbb{E}_p[\phi] - \mathbb{E}_q[\phi] \big).

Therefore, for an L-Lipschitz \phi, |\mathbb{E}_p[\phi] - \mathbb{E}_q[\phi]| \le L W_1(p,q).

J.4. Submodularity of mutual information. For finite observation sets, f(\mathcal{Z}) = I(X; Z_\mathcal{Z}) satisfies f(\mathcal{A}\cup\{s\}) - f(\mathcal{A}) \ge f(\mathcal{B}\cup\{s\}) - f(\mathcal{B}) when \mathcal{A}\subseteq\mathcal{B}, because conditioning reduces entropy.

J.5. Exact penalty for constrained MDPs. A smooth constraint g(b,c,a)\le 0 can be enforced by augmenting the reward with -M \max(0,g). As M\to\infty, the penalised value function converges uniformly to the constrained optimum; a finite threshold M^* exists if the value is bounded (Bertsekas, 2014, Chapter 8).

J.6. Advantage approximation error in approximate policy iteration. Munos (2003) shows that if the advantage function used for greedy action selection has pointwise error at most \varepsilon, the value loss of the resulting policy relative to the optimal is bounded by \frac{2\varepsilon}{(1-\gamma)^2}. The factor (1-\gamma)^{-2} captures compounding through both policy evaluation and policy improvement steps in the iteration. Our greedy FCV policy selects contacts using an approximate advantage within a truncated lookahead; the same bound applies to the suboptimality induced by the approximation.

